            <ArticleTitle>Leukocytosis at termination of antibiotic therapy: its importance for intra-abdominal sepsis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>918-21</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Postoperative outcomes of 31 afebrile patients who had responded to treatment for intra-abdominal sepsis were compared based on the presence or absence of leukocytosis (WBC count greater than 10,000/cu mm) at the conclusion of antibiotic therapy. In 68% of the patients who had leukocytosis, postoperative septic complications developed within two months of their operation. In patients without leukocytosis, complications developed in only 8.3%. Afebrile patients who exhibit leukocytosis but have responded clinically to treatment are at risk for postoperative infection and multisystem failure.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
